        "original_id": "d8870e25-ea72-5a10-9f08-6d2500e50337",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Ghosts, Battle for Haditha\n- Concepts: Gweilo\n- People: Nick Broomfield\n- Topics: Cantonese slang, racial language, documentary-style dramatization\n\nFacts:\n- The film \"Ghosts\" is titled with a reference to the Cantonese slang term \"Gweilo\" (鬼佬), which means \"ghost man.\"\n- The term \"Gweilo\" is used for white people.\n- In its unmodified form, \"Gweilo\" applies only to people of European ethnicities.\n- The term \"Gweilo\" has a history of racially deprecatory use.\n- \"Battle for Haditha\" dramatizes real events in a documentary style.\n- Nick Broomfield's film \"Battle for Haditha\" is his follow-up to \"Ghosts.\"",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4a081773-90c4-5312-92fb-d1c292dbaf97",
        "text": "Page: Ghosts (2006 film). Sentence: The title is a reference to the Cantonese slang term \"Gweilo\" (鬼佬), meaning \"ghost man\", used for white people.\nPage: Gweilo. Sentence: In its unmodified form, it applies only to European ethnicities and has a history of racially deprecatory use.\nPage: Battle for Haditha. Sentence: Dramatising real events using a documentary style, \"Battle for Haditha\" is Broomfield's follow up to \"Ghosts.\"",
